+# Hegel property-based testing report: diamond-types
+
+Property-based tests added with the [hegel](https://hegel.dev) Rust library (`hegeltest` 0.28.2)
+to the `diamond-types` text CRDT crate.
+
+Tests live alongside the code they exercise (per the skill's "modify existing test files" rule):
+
+- `src/list_fuzzer_tools.rs` — shared hegel generators (`hegel_tools` module): boundary-heavy
+  unicode char/content generators and hegel ports of `old_make_random_change`.
+- `src/list/list.rs` — local-edit model, time-travel, agent-independence properties.
+- `src/list/oplog_merge_fuzzer.rs` — convergence, idempotence, commutativity properties.
+- `src/list/encoding/tests.rs` — encode/decode roundtrip, options, patch composition, decode
+  robustness, plus two deterministic regression pins.
+
+## How to run
+
+The crate requires Rust >= 1.96 (uses `std::range::Range`), so the environment's default 1.95
+toolchain will not build it. Tests were run with:
+
+```
+cargo +1.97 test -p diamond-types --lib --features serde
+```
+
+`--features serde` is required to build *any* test of this crate: `src/causalgraph/graph/tools.rs`'s
+test module unconditionally imports `crate::serde_helpers`, which is `#[cfg(feature = "serde")]`.
+This is a pre-existing condition of the crate (the pristine tree fails `cargo test` without the
+feature) and is not caused by the hegel tests. CI (`.github/workflows/test.yml`) runs bare
+`cargo test`; that is a separate pre-existing issue, reported below but not fixed.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+All property tests are prefixed `hegel_`. Each is a single property (one property per test).
+
+### 1. CRDT convergence (crown property) — `hegel_concurrent_merge_converges`
+Three replicas share an agent table (agent id == replica index, so concurrent edits never reuse
+an `(agent, seq)` pair). Each round draws a batch of concurrent insert/delete ops spread across
+the replicas, then *sometimes* syncs a drawn pair (skipping lets replicas diverge deeper). At the
+end everyone syncs with everyone. Asserts: after exchanging oplogs both directions, the oplogs are
+`==`, and merging into each branch yields identical text. Evidence: the crate's own seeded
+`oplog_merge_fuzz` asserts exactly this. Explored at 1000 cases × up to 25 rounds × 8 changes.
+
+### 2. Local edits vs a String model — `hegel_local_edits_match_string_model`
+Applies a drawn edit script to a `ListCRDT` and to a naive `Vec<char>` reference document in
+lockstep; asserts identical text and char length after every op. This is the Tier-1 model test.
+Evidence: `ListCRDT` is documented as a live text document; `count_chars`/char-indexed positions
+are the crate's addressing model.
+
+### 3. Time travel / checkout — `hegel_checkout_reproduces_history`
+Records the version LV after each edit alongside the model text at that moment, then checks out
+each historical version and asserts the text matches. Also asserts `checkout(&[])` (root) is empty.
+Evidence: `OpLog::checkout` is documented as "Creating a checkout (snapshot) of the document at any
+requested point in time".
+
+### 4. Agent-assignment independence — `hegel_agent_assignment_doesnt_affect_content`
+Applies the same linear edit script to one single-agent document and one document that rotates the
+edit among three agents (including a unicode-named agent). Asserts identical content throughout.
+Evidence: the docs stress that agent IDs are opaque identity metadata, not content.
+
+### 5. Oplog encode→decode roundtrip — `hegel_encode_decode_roundtrip`
+Builds an oplog with concurrent history, encodes with full fidelity
+(`EncodeOptions::full().store_deleted_content(true)`), decodes, and asserts oplog equality and
+identical checkout text. Evidence: the crate's `fuzz_encode_decode_once`. The crate emphasises its
+compact binary format, so roundtrip is a prime property.
+
+### 6. Encode *configuration* generation — `hegel_encode_options_roundtrip`
+Generates the `EncodeOptions` flags themselves (`store_start_branch_content`,
+`store_inserted_content`, `store_deleted_content`, `compress_content`, optional `user_data`) rather
+than testing one canned config. Asserts the causal graph always roundtrips; text roundtrips when
+inserted content was stored; the full oplog roundtrips when both contents were stored. This follows
+the skill's "generate configurations, not just payloads" guidance.
+
+### 7. Incremental patch composition — `hegel_encode_patch_composes`
+Encodes a snapshot, makes more edits, encodes a patch via `encode_from(snapshot_version)`, then
+reassembles snapshot + patch via `decode_and_add` and asserts equality with the monolithic oplog
+and identical text. Evidence: `decode_in_parts` / `merge_parts` tests.
+
+### 8. Merge idempotence — `hegel_oplog_merge_idempotent`
+Asserts `add_missing_operations_from` is idempotent: merging the same remote ops a second time, and
+merging an oplog with itself, both leave it unchanged. Evidence: the method is documented "Any
+duplicate operations are ignored" — a CRDT merge must be idempotent.
+
+### 9. Merge commutativity — `hegel_oplog_merge_commutes`
+Asserts merging remote oplogs b-then-c equals c-then-b (oplog equality and identical checkout text).
+Evidence: CRDT merge must be order-independent; the seeded fuzzer relies on this implicitly.
+
+### 10. Corrupt-decode contract — `hegel_corrupt_decode_errors_or_unrolls`
+Applies drawn corruptions (byte flips, truncation, garbage splices) to a valid encoding and decodes
+into a target that already knows a prefix. The contract (`error_unrolling` test + the doc comment on
+`decode_and_add_opts`): a failed decode must unroll, a successful decode must be internally
+consistent. This property caught two real bugs (below); it now skips the panic bug (via
+`catch_unwind`) and asserts the weaker "user-visible state unchanged" for the unroll bug, so it
+keeps exercising the rest of the input space while the two bugs are pinned separately.
+
+### 11. Decode robustness / parse no-panic — `hegel_load_from_garbage_never_panics`
+Feeds `ListOpLog::load_from` random bytes (half prefixed with the real file magic). The property:
+never panic; if it parses, the result is internally consistent. (Currently skips the known panic
+bug via `catch_unwind`.)
+
+## Test results
+
+All properties pass at the default (100) case count and at a 10x exploratory run, **except the two
+deterministic regression pins**, which fail on every run by design (they document real bugs — see
+below). Liveness was verified by inverting `hegel_local_edits_match_string_model`'s length assertion
+and confirming hegel reported the minimal shrunk counterexample (`num_ops = 1`, insert `'\0'`), then
+reverting.
+
+```
+running 13 tests
+test list::encoding::tests::hegel_pinned_decode_corrupted_bytes_panics ... FAILED   (pinned bug #1)
+test list::encoding::tests::hegel_pinned_failed_decode_must_unroll     ... FAILED   (pinned bug #2)
+test list::encoding::tests::hegel_load_from_garbage_never_panics       ... ok
+test list::encoding::tests::hegel_corrupt_decode_errors_or_unrolls     ... ok
+test list::encoding::tests::hegel_encode_decode_roundtrip              ... ok
+test list::encoding::tests::hegel_encode_options_roundtrip             ... ok
+test list::encoding::tests::hegel_encode_patch_composes                ... ok
+test list::oplog_merge_fuzzer::hegel_concurrent_merge_converges        ... ok
+test list::oplog_merge_fuzzer::hegel_oplog_merge_idempotent            ... ok
+test list::oplog_merge_fuzzer::hegel_oplog_merge_commutes              ... ok
+test list::list::tests::hegel_local_edits_match_string_model           ... ok
+test list::list::tests::hegel_checkout_reproduces_history              ... ok
+test list::list::tests::hegel_agent_assignment_doesnt_affect_content   ... ok
+```
+
+The full `--lib` suite: 149 passed, 2 failed (the pins), 20 ignored.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+Both are in the binary decoder and are reachable from **untrusted input via public API**
+(`ListOpLog::load_from` / `decode_and_add`). Not fixed, per instructions. Each has a deterministic
+regression pin that reproduces by exhaustively corrupting a minimal encoded document, so it fails on
+every run rather than probabilistically.
+
+### Bug 1 — decoder panics on malformed input instead of returning `ParseError`
+Pin: `hegel_pinned_decode_corrupted_bytes_panics`.
+
+Minimal counterexample (found by hegel, then reduced): encode a one-character document (insert
+`'\0'`), splice a single byte into the encoding, and call `ListOpLog::load_from`. Six single-byte
+splices near offset 43-47 (into the history chunk) cause a panic.
+
+Code path:
+`ListOpLog::load_from` → `decode_internal` (`src/list/encoding/decode_oplog.rs:889`) →
+`history_entry_map_and_truncate` (`decode_oplog.rs:242`) →
+`RleVec::find_packed_with_offset` → `Option::unwrap()` on `None` (`src/rle/rle_vec.rs:191`).
+
+Root cause: a corrupted history chunk references a local time that has no entry in the version map
+built while reading the patches chunk; `find_packed_with_offset` assumes the needle is present and
+unwraps. The file CRC does **not** protect against this: the CRC chunk is verified only at the *end*
+of `decode_internal` (`decode_oplog.rs:949`), after all chunks are already parsed, so any panic
+reachable during parsing is reachable from untrusted bytes. `load_from` returns `Result` precisely
+to signal malformed input, so panicking is a contract violation and a DoS vector for anyone decoding
+peer-supplied data.
+
+### Bug 2 — failed `decode_and_add` does not fully unroll (corrupts oplog agent state)
+Pin: `hegel_pinned_failed_decode_must_unroll`.
+
+`decode_and_add_opts` documents (`decode_oplog.rs:476`): "when an error happens we need to unwind all
+the merged operations before returning. Otherwise self is in an invalid state." The existing
+`error_unrolling` test verifies this only for a **fresh, empty** target oplog. When the target
+**already knows a prefix** of the incoming operations, the unwind is incomplete.
+
+Minimal counterexample: oplog A holds one op (insert `'\0'`); encode an extension of A (A + one
+delete); flip one byte so the decode fails on the CRC check; `A.decode_and_add(...)` returns `Err`
+but leaves `A` modified — `client_data[agent].lv_for_seq` covers seq range `0..2` while `A.len()`
+is still 1. Nine distinct single-byte flips trigger it. The oplog is left claiming a seq maps to a
+local version that does not exist, so subsequent edits by that agent can silently collide (the
+exact "reuse IDs → divergence" hazard the crate's own docs warn about).
+
+Code path: the unwind loop in `decode_and_add_opts` (`decode_oplog.rs:500`) trims
+`client_with_lv`, `operations`, and history back to the pre-decode length, but does not correctly
+restore each agent's `lv_for_seq` mapping for ops that were newly assigned during the failed decode.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+None were abandoned as unsound. One property (`hegel_corrupt_decode_errors_or_unrolls`) was
+**weakened rather than deleted** once it exposed the two bugs above: it now skips the panic bug via
+`catch_unwind` and asserts only the user-visible invariant (`version` and `len` unchanged) for the
+unroll bug, with comments pointing at the canonical pins. This is the skill's sanctioned pattern —
+pin a bug with one canonical failing test, then exclude that known-buggy behaviour from sibling
+properties so they keep testing everything else. The strict form of both invariants lives in the
+two pins.
+
+The convergence/idempotence/commutativity properties merge oplogs by cloning one argument to satisfy
+the borrow checker; this does not weaken them because `add_missing_operations_from` only reads its
+argument.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- **MSRV vs environment toolchain.** The crate pins `rust-version = 1.96`; the environment's default
+  is 1.95, so the crate does not build at all with the default `cargo`. `cargo add --dev hegeltest`
+  succeeded (it does not enforce the crate's own MSRV against the *active* toolchain here), but every
+  subsequent `cargo test` failed on `std::range::Range` until switched to `cargo +1.97`. The
+  reference's "MSRV resolution error" note is about hegeltest's MSRV being *higher* than the target;
+  this is the reverse (target's MSRV higher than the default toolchain) and worth a sentence in the
+  Setup/Gotchas section: check `rust-version` in Cargo.toml and pick a new-enough `cargo +toolchain`
+  before assuming a build failure is your fault.
+- **Feature-gated test-only imports.** This crate's non-hegel test code (`serde_helpers`) is behind
+  a non-default feature, so *no* test target builds without `--features serde`. An agent that runs
+  bare `cargo test -p <crate>` sees a wall of errors unrelated to its own tests and can misdiagnose.
+  A skill note like "if the pristine `cargo test` already fails to compile, find the feature the
+  existing tests need before adding yours" would help. (I confirmed the failure exists on the
+  pristine tree via `git stash`.)
+- **The `catch_unwind` skip pattern worked well** for keeping a broad property alive while pinning a
+  panic bug separately, but the skill only describes ignoring/skipping the *whole* reproducer test
+  for process-aborting bugs. For a merely-*panicking* (unwinding) bug, wrapping the call in
+  `catch_unwind` inside a sibling property — so the sibling keeps exercising non-panicking inputs —
+  is a useful middle ground worth mentioning alongside the "exclude known-buggy input from siblings"
+  advice.
+- Everything else in the Rust reference was accurate: `#[hegel::test]`, `one_of!`,
+  `sampled_from(vec)`, `generators::optional`, `generators::binary`, the codepoint-drawing idiom for
+  chars, and the "boost + raise test_cases / make deterministic" guidance for pinned bugs all worked
+  as documented.
